Z||000011||Guinea Pig||05-18-2000||01:10 AM||||Loafer, I can understand your frustration.  After returning from the Nationals in St. Louis (Collensville, Il.)and having no problems going there, while there or returning home, I was notified by the local police that someone had broken out the rear window of my GT.  That is a bear of a window to replace.  <P>Instead of venting your anger in a violent manner that will probably cause you more legal grief than you want to deal with, if lighting and location permit, replace the bat with a video camera.  Get them on film.  Call the police, preferably while your filming.  Realize that they may have some "tool" that they are using to break into your car.  They can use it against you.  And if it is a "they", bat or no, you are out numbered.  Also realize, any injuries caused by you, even though they are involved in a criminal act, can bring criminal as well as civil action against you.  Just my $.02 as a retired police officer.           ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||05-18-2000||01:17 AM||||It is against the law to booby trap your car. Here's an Idea a friend and I did when I noticed someone trying to break into my car. (he ran away) but we took turns at night watching the neighborhood. A few days later we notice these kids breaking into cars. We followed them around and watched them stash the stuff in their car. When they went back out to "collect" more stuff we super glued the door locks and called the police. A detective came around and we sat waiting. When they showed up to get into the car, The cops nailed them! They had a over dozen stereo's along with radar detectors, cell phones, tapes, and CD's! Of course since they damaged your car I would just find their car and torch it!||||reg||1
